1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,139
Every engagement dashboard you've ever seen treats more usage as unambiguously good.

2
00:00:05,139 --> 00:00:09,758
Traditional software costs almost nothing per extra use, an extra page view or an

3
00:00:09,758 --> 00:00:14,262
extra saved document, so a feature that gets used more is simply a feature that

4
00:00:14,262 --> 00:00:15,459
succeeded, full stop.

5
00:00:15,459 --> 00:00:19,565
AI features don't, and that one difference changes what "success" means.

6
00:00:19,565 --> 00:00:24,126
Every single use has a real, measurable cost, the one you built in 7.2, and that

7
00:00:24,126 --> 00:00:26,350
cost scales up exactly when usage does.

8
00:00:26,350 --> 00:00:28,345
That's the trap this lecture names.

9
00:00:28,345 --> 00:00:32,849
A feature can pass every row of last lecture's scorecard, launch with a healthy

10
00:00:32,849 --> 00:00:37,639
margin, and still lose money on its most active users the moment they use it enough.

11
00:00:37,639 --> 00:00:42,257
Nobody put that number in the launch deck, because nobody was looking at cost and

12
00:00:42,257 --> 00:00:43,683
revenue on the same page.

13
00:00:43,683 --> 00:00:48,358
Picture the support-ticket summariser this course keeps returning to, scaled up to

14
00:00:48,358 --> 00:00:50,981
the desk that clears the most tickets a month.

15
00:00:50,981 --> 00:00:55,656
That desk looks like the retention win on every dashboard, right up until the same

16
00:00:55,656 --> 00:01:00,217
feature's cost line quietly crosses its allocated revenue for exactly that user.

17
00:01:00,217 --> 00:01:04,893
Most teams are still running the old, safe intuition about what heavy usage means.

18
00:01:05,443 --> 00:01:10,179
Read the margin column down, not the cost column, because the cost column looks fine

19
00:01:10,179 --> 00:01:11,306
at every single row.

20
00:01:11,306 --> 00:01:15,647
Eight cents, a dollar sixty eight, six dollars thirty, all individually small

21
00:01:15,647 --> 00:01:18,297
numbers that don't sound alarming on their own.

22
00:01:18,297 --> 00:01:20,665
The margin column tells a different story.

23
00:01:20,665 --> 00:01:25,345
Somewhere between forty and a hundred and fifty conversations a month, this feature

24
00:01:25,345 --> 00:01:29,855
crosses from genuinely profitable to actively losing money, on the exact users a

25
00:01:29,855 --> 00:01:32,674
product team would normally celebrate as its best.

26
00:01:32,674 --> 00:01:35,098
That crossover point is the actual finding.

27
00:01:35,098 --> 00:01:38,819
Everything else in this table is just arithmetic leading up to it.

28
00:01:38,819 --> 00:01:42,259
Find your own crossover point rather than borrowing this one.

29
00:01:42,259 --> 00:01:46,882
It moves with your actual per-conversation cost and your actual allocated revenue,

30
00:01:46,882 --> 00:01:51,505
and a feature with thinner margins per user or a higher cost per conversation will

31
00:01:51,505 --> 00:01:54,549
cross into loss much earlier than a hundred and fifty.

32
00:01:54,549 --> 00:01:59,116
The shape of the table matters more than these specific three rows: cost climbing

33
00:01:59,116 --> 00:02:02,668
steadily while revenue holds flat, until one crosses the other.

34
00:02:03,218 --> 00:02:07,890
Both dashboards are describing the identical user, honestly, from the data each one

35
00:02:07,890 --> 00:02:08,904
was built to show.

36
00:02:08,904 --> 00:02:09,861
Neither is lying.

37
00:02:09,861 --> 00:02:14,477
They're just answering different questions, and only one of them is the question a

38
00:02:14,477 --> 00:02:16,616
margin review actually needs answered.

39
00:02:16,616 --> 00:02:21,232
That's worth sitting with, because it means nobody has to be careless or dishonest

40
00:02:21,232 --> 00:02:22,583
for this trap to spring.

41
00:02:22,583 --> 00:02:26,298
Every dashboard involved is doing exactly what it was built to do.

42
00:02:26,298 --> 00:02:30,746
The failure is architectural, not personal: a metrics system with no page where

43
00:02:30,746 --> 00:02:33,054
usage and cost sit next to the same user.

44
00:02:33,054 --> 00:02:37,613
This is precisely why the two numbers need to live on the same page, the way last

45
00:02:37,613 --> 00:02:42,342
lecture's scorecard put quality, cost, and speed together instead of leaving them in

46
00:02:42,342 --> 00:02:45,157
three separate documents nobody cross- references.

47
00:02:45,157 --> 00:02:49,773
An engagement win that's quietly a margin loss stays invisible for exactly as long

48
00:02:49,773 --> 00:02:51,574
as those two numbers never meet.

49
00:02:51,574 --> 00:02:55,177
Who in your organisation actually owns that meeting point today?

50
00:02:55,177 --> 00:02:57,767
Product usually owns the engagement dashboard.

51
00:02:57,767 --> 00:03:02,045
Finance usually owns the margin conversation, if anyone does, and often only

52
00:03:02,045 --> 00:03:06,436
discovers it quarterly, long after a cohort of power users has already formed.

53
00:03:06,436 --> 00:03:10,939
The gap between those two owners is exactly where this trap survives undetected.

54
00:03:11,489 --> 00:03:16,102
Say the honest caveat clearly, because the tempting wrong conclusion here is that

55
00:03:16,102 --> 00:03:17,639
heavy usage is the problem.

56
00:03:17,639 --> 00:03:18,152
It isn't.

57
00:03:18,152 --> 00:03:20,258
Heavy usage proved the feature works.

58
00:03:20,258 --> 00:03:24,871
The problem is a flat-rate structure that was never designed to track a cost that

59
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:25,497
isn't flat.

60
00:03:25,497 --> 00:03:30,109
The real fix looks like tiered plans, a usage-based add-on past a threshold, or a

61
00:03:30,109 --> 00:03:34,836
generous-but-real cap with a clear upgrade path, not a feature quietly punished for

62
00:03:34,836 --> 00:03:35,462
succeeding.

63
00:03:35,462 --> 00:03:39,790
Whichever fix you pick, design it before the power-user tier exists in large

64
00:03:39,790 --> 00:03:44,459
numbers, because retrofitting pricing onto users who are already used to unlimited

65
00:03:44,459 --> 00:03:47,989
is a far harder conversation than shipping it with the launch.

66
00:03:47,989 --> 00:03:52,715
Frame the cap generously enough that it never touches a genuinely typical user, and

67
00:03:52,715 --> 00:03:56,986
specifically enough that the crossover point from this lecture's table sits

68
00:03:56,986 --> 00:03:58,182
comfortably above it.

69
00:03:58,182 --> 00:04:00,460
A cap set from a guess protects nothing.

70
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:05,186
A cap set from your own measured crossover protects the feature's economics without

71
00:04:05,186 --> 00:04:07,179
most users ever noticing it exists.

72
00:04:07,729 --> 00:04:11,867
That's the definition worth carrying past today, and it's worth carrying

73
00:04:11,867 --> 00:04:14,339
specifically because it's counterintuitive.

74
00:04:14,339 --> 00:04:16,868
More usage is supposed to mean more success.

75
00:04:16,868 --> 00:04:21,236
For a feature with real marginal cost, past a certain point, it can mean the

76
00:04:21,236 --> 00:04:21,753
opposite.

77
00:04:21,753 --> 00:04:25,260
Run the exercise on someone real, not a hypothetical average.

78
00:04:25,260 --> 00:04:29,973
Averages hide exactly this crossover, because they blend your light users and your

79
00:04:29,973 --> 00:04:34,743
power users into one comfortable-looking number that no single real person actually

80
00:04:34,743 --> 00:04:34,973
has.

81
00:04:34,973 --> 00:04:39,514
A blended average margin that looks healthy can be sitting directly on top of a

82
00:04:39,514 --> 00:04:44,170
profitable majority and a loss-making tail, and the average alone will never tell

83
00:04:44,170 --> 00:04:45,319
you the tail exists.

84
00:04:45,319 --> 00:04:50,033
Segment by actual usage before you trust any single margin number presented as the

85
00:04:50,033 --> 00:04:50,837
whole picture.

86
00:04:51,387 --> 00:04:53,036
One thing before you move on.

87
00:04:53,036 --> 00:04:57,585
Find a real power user, not an estimate, and run their actual usage through your

88
00:04:57,585 --> 00:04:58,210
cost model.

89
00:04:58,210 --> 00:05:02,645
If nobody can produce that number quickly, that's itself the finding: cost and

90
00:05:02,645 --> 00:05:06,682
engagement have never been checked against each other for this feature.

91
00:05:06,682 --> 00:05:09,752
Then look honestly at today's pricing or usage limits.

92
00:05:09,752 --> 00:05:14,528
If neither one moves as usage climbs, that gap is worth raising in the next planning

93
00:05:14,528 --> 00:05:15,267
conversation.

94
00:05:15,267 --> 00:05:19,816
This section's next lecture, latency budgets, will matter to you either way, but

95
00:05:19,816 --> 00:05:24,308
this margin question is the one worth raising first, before a power-user cohort

96
00:05:24,308 --> 00:05:27,605
makes it an expensive conversation instead of a cheap one.
